{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.TorusKnotMass","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.TorusKnotMass","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":14,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.TorusKnotMass.two_distinct_torus_mass_classes","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.TorusKnotMass.two_distinct_torus_mass_classes","kind":"theorem","line":109,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/TorusKnotMass.lean#L109-L114","doc":"The torus-knot mass classes form a 2-element type:\n{gauge bosons, scalar}. The `factors_distinct` field ensures\nthese are genuinely different. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.TorusKnotMass.higgs_to_gauge_factor_ratio_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.TorusKnotMass.higgs_to_gauge_factor_ratio_pos","kind":"theorem","line":103,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/TorusKnotMass.lean#L103-L107","doc":"The ratio is positive (both factors are positive). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.TorusKnotMass.higgs_to_gauge_factor_ratio","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.TorusKnotMass.higgs_to_gauge_factor_ratio","kind":"def","line":98,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/TorusKnotMass.lean#L98-L101","doc":"The Higgs-to-gauge ratio: how the scalar mass factor relates to\nthe gauge mass factor. Empirically ≈ 171/143 ≈ 1.196. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.TorusKnotMass.WZ_mass_ratio_in_unit","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.TorusKnotMass.WZ_mass_ratio_in_unit","kind":"theorem","line":92,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/TorusKnotMass.lean#L92-L92","doc":"The W/Z ratio is in (0, 1) (W is lighter than Z). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.TorusKnotMass.WZ_mass_ratio","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.TorusKnotMass.WZ_mass_ratio","kind":"def","line":90,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/TorusKnotMass.lean#L90-L90","doc":"The Weinberg-angle-equivalent ratio: $m_W / m_Z = $ cos θ_W.\nFor torus knots W = T(2,4), Z = T(2,6), this should equal the\nratio of their trace polynomials $P_2(h_0) / P_3(h_0)$ when\nboth use the same `gauge_mass_factor`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.TorusKnotMass.factors_distinct","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.TorusKnotMass.factors_distinct","kind":"theorem","line":84,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/TorusKnotMass.lean#L84-L84","doc":"The two mass factors are distinct: gauge bosons and scalars\nhave structurally different categorical normalizations. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.TorusKnotMass.scalar_mass_factor_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.TorusKnotMass.scalar_mass_factor_pos","kind":"theorem","line":80,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/TorusKnotMass.lean#L80-L80","doc":"Scalar mass factor is positive. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.TorusKnotMass.scalar_mass_factor","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.TorusKnotMass.scalar_mass_factor","kind":"def","line":78,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/TorusKnotMass.lean#L78-L78","doc":"Multiplicative factor in the mass formula for the **Higgs scalar** (H).\nEmpirically (probe `rt_binomial_trace_evaluation_probe.py`):\n`scalar_mass_factor ≈ 171` (matches `mass(H)/P_m(h_0)` within 1%). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.TorusKnotMass.gauge_mass_factor_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.TorusKnotMass.gauge_mass_factor_pos","kind":"theorem","line":73,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/TorusKnotMass.lean#L73-L73","doc":"Gauge mass factor is positive. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.TorusKnotMass.gauge_mass_factor","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.TorusKnotMass.gauge_mass_factor","kind":"def","line":71,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/TorusKnotMass.lean#L71-L71","doc":"Multiplicative factor in the mass formula for **gauge bosons**\n(W, Z). Empirically (probe `rt_binomial_trace_evaluation_probe.py`):\n`gauge_mass_factor ≈ 143` (matches `mass(W)/P_m(h_0)` and\n`mass(Z)/P_m(h_0)` within 1%). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.TorusKnotMass.poly_growth_degree_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.TorusKnotMass.poly_growth_degree_eq","kind":"theorem","line":65,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/TorusKnotMass.lean#L65-L65","doc":"The degree formula: $\\\\deg = n - 1$ for $T(2, n)$. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.TorusKnotMass.poly_growth_degree","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.TorusKnotMass.poly_growth_degree","kind":"def","line":63,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/TorusKnotMass.lean#L63-L63","doc":"Polynomial growth rate of $|J_N(T(2, n))|$ in $N$.\nFor torus knots, the Kashaev limit is zero (Vol = 0), so\ngrowth must be sub-exponential. The polynomial degree is\n`n - 1` for T(2, n) (heuristic; confirmed by empirical\nprobes for n = 4, 6, 8). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.TorusKnotMass.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.TorusKnotMass.mk","kind":"ctor","line":57,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/TorusKnotMass.lean#L57-L57","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.TorusKnotMass","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.TorusKnotMass","kind":"class","line":46,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/TorusKnotMass.lean#L46-L92","doc":"**Torus-knot mass class** — extends `VolumeConjecture` with\nfields for the polynomial growth rate of $|J_N(T(2, n))|$ and the\nmass-formula split between gauge bosons and scalars.\n\nThe empirical W/Z = cos θ_W result (probe `rt_binomial_trace_evaluation_probe.py`,\nPR #1128) is captured here as a TARGET that a complete instance\nwould establish.\n\nPer AGENTS.md §3b-tax: the class fields encode the conjectural\ncontent; the **absence of an instance** is the open structural\nstatement. ","signatureChunk":0}]}